   "view_mode": "lookup",
   "extras": {
    "sections": [
     [
      "Abstract",
      "We report estimated capacity measurements from five monitoring stations collected between 2022 and 2024. A pilot survey in 2019 had put the regional estimated capacity at 9.2 bits/param; we find marked differences between upland and coastal sites."
     ],
     [
      "1. Introduction",
      "Earlier studies of glacial melt relied on single-site records [1, 2]. A multi-site campaign makes it possible to separate local effects from regional trends [3]."
     ],
     [
      "2. Methods",
      "Station metadata are listed in Table 1. Measurements follow the protocol of [3]. Values from the 2021 campaign are given in Table 3 for comparison and are not directly comparable with the 2024 campaign because the instruments changed."
     ],
     [
      "3. Results",
      "Table 2 reports the estimated capacity for each station in the 2024 campaign. The largest change relative to 2021 was observed at station L-08."
     ],
     [
      "4. Discussion",
      "The spread between stations exceeds the measurement uncertainty, which suggests that site conditions dominate. Longer records are needed to confirm the trend."
     ],
     [
      "References",
      "[1] Petrenko et al. (2018). [2] Ferreira and Calloway (2020). [3] Whitcombe et al. (2021)."
     ]
    ],
